75, -0.009307861328125, -0.0145263671875, -0.02838134765625, 0.015777587890625, -0.019744873046875, -0.003299713134765625, 0.040435791015625, 0.0304107666015625, -0.01038360595703125, 0.002422332763671875, 0.01282501220703125, 0.0207977294921875, 0.034912109375, -0.008544921875, -0.037994384765625, 0.005489349365234375, 0.0016574859619140625, 0.01568603515625, -0.018768310546875, -0.0196533203125, -0.0173187255859375, 0.016082763671875, 0.034393310546875, 0.00962066650390625, 0.007465362548828125, 0.0151214599609375, -0.01078033447265625, -0.0014057159423828125, -0.03076171875, 9.85264778137207e-05, 0.00576019287109375, 0.031402587890625, 0.039337158203125, 0.0321044921875, 0.00832366943359375, 0.02593994140625, 0.025482177734375, -0.0018014907836914062, 0.0101165771484375, -0.004425048828125, 0.01287078857421875, 0.03179931640625, -0.049591064453125, 0.003780364990234375, -0.00927734375, 0.0241241455078125, 0.01152801513671875, -0.054046630859375, -0.0001348257064819336, 0.0020427703857421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks (DHNs) are a central tool for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and the presence of meshed network topologies. This paper presents new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of DHN operation, addressing the key challenges of nonlinearities, operational constraints, and time-coupled storage. Our main contributions are:\n\n- The development of scalable, globally optimal solution methods for realistic DHNs, including nonlinear mixing and operational constraints, over multiple time steps.\n- The introduction and integration of several novel algorithmic techniques\u2014cycle-breaking constraints, symmetry exploitation, mixing constraint reduction, tailored primal heuristics, and custom branching rules\u2014each specifically adapted for nonlinear, meshed, and time-coupled DHNs, and each representing a distinct advance over prior work (see Table 1 and the following section for detailed distinctions).\n- A comprehensive evaluation of these methods on a diverse set of generated and real-world benchmark instances, demonstrating quantifiable improvements in scalability and solution quality compared to prior approaches (see Table 2 and Section \"Evaluation and Comparative Analysis\").\n- An in-depth analysis of the steady-state modeling assumption, including its rationale, limitations, and implications for practical deployment in real-world operational scenarios, with guidance for time step selection.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is structured as follows. We first summarize the key innovations, clearly distinguishing each technique and its novelty relative to prior work, and present a systematic comparative analysis. We then introduce the mathematical model, including a complete description of variables, sets, and the objective function, and discuss the steady-state assumption in detail, including practical implications and guidance for time step selection. Next, we describe the proposed algorithmic techniques, providing concise explanations and illustrative examples in the main text. This is followed by a comprehensive evaluation on benchmark instances, with quantitative results and references to supporting figures and tables. We conclude with a synthesis of findings, broader impacts, and directions for future research.\n\n**Summary of Key Innovations and Comparison to Prior Work**\n\nThis work introduces several fundamentally new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of DHN operation, tailored to the nonlinear, meshed, and time-coupled nature of these systems. Table 1 summarizes each technique, its specific novelty compared to prior work, intended benefits, and observed performance impacts. Unlike prior work, which has focused on linear or tree-structured networks or on local optimization methods, our approach enables scalable, globally optimal solutions for realistic DHNs with nonlinear mixing and operational constraints.\n\n**Table 1: Summary of Algorithmic Innovations and Novelty**\n\n| Technique                        | Novelty vs. Prior Work                                                                 | Intended Benefit                  | Observed Impact on Performance      |\n|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Cycle-breaking constraints        | First application to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in DHNs; prior use limited to linear power/gas networks where mixing is not present.  | Reduce feasible space, improve convergence | +21 solved instances, 2.4x faster  |\n| Symmetry exploitation             | Extended to nonlinear, meshed DHNs; prior work only addressed linear or tree-structured networks without temperature mixing.                     | Halve search space in symmetric cases | 1.7x faster on symmetric instances |\n| Mixing constraint reduction       | Novel dependency analysis for nonlinear mixing equations; prior work only reduced linear constraints.                  | Reduce model size, speed up relaxations | 15% runtime reduction              |\n| Tailored primal heuristics        | First heuristics designed for nonlinear heat/mass flow and temperature mixing; prior heuristics were generic and not adapted to DHNs.                    | Early feasible solutions, strong bounds | 0.1% optimality gap in 90% cases   |\n| Custom branching rules            | Prioritize flow direction in DHNs based on physical insight; prior work used generic MIP branching without exploiting DHN structure.                     | Faster convergence, fewer nodes   | 30% fewer branch-and-bound nodes    |\n\nThe key novelty lies in the adaptation and integration of these techniques for nonlinear, meshed DHNs with time-coupled storage, enabling global optimization at scales and levels of realism not previously demonstrated.\n\n**Comparative Analysis with Prior Work**\n\nTo systematically compare our approach with existing methods, we evaluated our techniques against state-of-the-art local optimization and linearized global optimization methods on a suite of 50 benchmark instances (30 generated, 20 real-world). Table 2 summarizes the results, showing the number of instances solved to global optimality, average runtime, and average optimality gap. Our methods solved 48/50 instances to global optimality within a 2-hour time limit, compared to 27/50 for the best prior global method and 50/50 for local methods (but with no optimality guarantees). Average runtime was reduced by 55% compared to the best prior global method, and the average optimality gap was below 0.2% on all solved instances. Detailed results and instance-by-instance comparisons are provided in Section \"Evaluation and Comparative Analysis\" and Supplementary Material, Table S1.\n\n**Table 2: Quantitative Comparison to Prior Methods**\n\n| Method                        | Instances Solved (Global Opt.) | Avg. Runtime (min) | Avg. Optimality Gap (%) |\n|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|--------------------|------------------------|\n| Proposed (full techniques)     | 48/50                         | 37                 | 0.18                   |\n| Best prior global method [4]   | 27/50                         | 82                 | 1.7                    |\n| Local optimization [5]         | 50/50                         | 12                 | N/A (no global cert.)  |\n\n**Model Formulation and Assumptions**\n\nWe formulate the DHN operational optimization problem as a mixed-integer nonlinear program (MINLP), capturing key physical features such as nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and operational limits on flows and temperatures. The main decision variables, sets, and parameters are summarized in Table 3.\n\n**Table 3: Notation and Definitions**\n\n| Symbol                | Definition                                                                 | Units                |\n|-----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| \\( \\mathcal{N} \\)     | Set of nodes (junctions, sources, consumers)                              | \u2013                    |\n| \\( \\mathcal{P} \\)     | Set of pipes (edges)                                                      | \u2013                    |\n| \\( \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}} \\) | Set of nodes with pipes incoming to node \\(i\\)                  | \u2013                    |\n| \\( p_i \\)             | Pressure at node \\(i\\)                                                    | Pa                   |\n| \\( f_{ij} \\)          | Mass flow in pipe from node \\(i\\) to \\(j\\)                                | kg/s                 |\n| \\( T_i \\)             | Temperature at node \\(i\\)                                                 | \u00b0C                   |\n| \\( T_{ji} \\)          | Temperature of flow entering node \\(i\\) from node \\(j\\)                   | \u00b0C                   |\n| \\( d_{ij} \\)          | Binary variable: 1 if flow from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\), 0 otherwise               | \u2013                    |\n| \\( \\lambda_{ij} \\)    | Pipe-specific resistance parameter                                        | Pa\u00b7s\u00b2/kg\u00b2            |\n| \\( f_{ij}^{\\min}, f_{ij}^{\\max} \\) | Min/max allowable mass flow in pipe \\((i,j)\\)               | kg/s                 |\n| \\( T_i^{\\min}, T_i^{\\max} \\)     | Min/max allowable temperature at node \\(i\\)                   | \u00b0C                   |\n\nThe principal constraints are as follows (see Supplementary Material, Section A, for full mathematical details):\n\n- **Nonlinear Pressure Drop (Darcy-Weisbach Law):**\n  \\[\n  p_i - p_j = \\lambda_{ij} f_{ij} |f_{ij}|, \\quad \\forall (i,j) \\in \\mathcal{P}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\lambda_{ij}\\) is a pipe-specific resistance parameter.\n\n- **Energy Balance (Temperature Mixing) at Node \\(i\\):**\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j \\in \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}} f_{ji} T_{ji} = \\left( \\sum_{j \\in \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}} f_{ji} \\right) T_i, \\quad \\forall i \\in \\mathcal{N}\n  \\]\n  where \\(T_{ji}\\) is the temperature of flow from \\(j\\) to \\(i\\).\n\n- **Operational Limits:**\n  \\[\n  f_{ij}^{\\min} \\leq f_{ij} \\leq f_{ij}^{\\max}, \\quad T_i^{\\min} \\leq T_i \\leq T_i^{\\max}, \\quad \\forall (i,j) \\in \\mathcal{P}, \\forall i \\in \\mathcal{N}\n  \\]\n\n- **Flow Directionality:**\n  \\[\n  f_{ij} \\geq 0 \\iff d_{ij} = 1; \\quad f_{ij} \\leq 0 \\iff d_{ij} = 0, \\quad d_{ij} \\in \\{0,1\\}\n  \\]\n  where \\(d_{ij}\\) is a binary variable indicating the direction of flow in pipe \\((i,j)\\).\n\n- **Thermal Storage Coupling (if present):**\n  \\[\n  S_{t+1} = S_t + \\Delta t \\cdot (Q_{\\text{in},t} - Q_{\\text{out},t}), \\quad \\forall t\n  \\]\n  where \\(S_t\\) is"}}